---
import BaseLayout from '@/layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About"
  description="Runtime Signals connects research claims, runtime invariants, operational evidence, and reproducible artifacts."
  canonicalPath="/about"
>
  <article class="prose">
    <p class="eyebrow">About</p>
    <h1>What Runtime Signals is for</h1>
    <p>
      Runtime Signals is not an AI news site. It publishes system-level analysis of evaluation,
      reliability, recovery, orchestration, state, memory, tool security, distributed execution,
      observability, and failure analysis for AI agents running in production.
    </p>
    <p>
      Most coverage of agent systems is either research summary or product announcement. Neither
      connects the four layers that actually determine whether a system holds up under load:
      research claims and their limitations, the runtime invariants that get violated (idempotency,
      leases, fencing, authorization, state transfer), the operational evidence — traces, costs,
      failures, recovery behavior — and reproducible implementation artifacts a reader can run or
      inspect.
    </p>
    <p>
      The editorial unit here is a chain: a real failure mechanism, the invariant it violates, and
      the engineering practice that holds under load. That's narrower than general AI commentary,
      and it's the whole point.
    </p>
    <h2>What we commit to</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>No unreviewed AI-generated articles are published.</li>
      <li>
        Every consequential factual claim has a source record or is explicitly marked inference or
        opinion.
      </li>
      <li>
        Source records preserve URL, publisher, publication date, access date, evidence type, and
        what the source supports.
      </li>
      <li>Articles state limitations and workload or configuration boundaries.</li>
      <li>
        A human approves publication, meaningful updates, corrections, and syndicated versions.
      </li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      See <a href="/methodology">methodology</a> for how claims are labeled and sourced, and <a
        href="/editorial-policy">editorial policy</a
      > for the review process itself.
    </p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .prose {
    max-width: var(--content-width);
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: var(--space-6) var(--space-5) var(--space-9);
    line-height: var(--leading-relaxed);
  }
  .prose h1 {
    font-family: var(--font-body);
    font-size: var(--text-2xl);
    margin: var(--space-2) 0 var(--space-5);
  }
  .prose h2 {
    font-family: var(--font-body);
    font-size: var(--text-xl);
    margin-top: var(--space-7);
  }
  .prose li {
    margin-bottom: var(--space-2);
  }
</style>
